-- title:
--   Adic completeness for a principal maximal ideal gives a DVR
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain, and let $\varpi \in R$ be an element with $\varpi \neq 0$. Assume that $R$ is complete for the $I$-adic topology, where $I = (\varpi)$ is the ideal generated by $\varpi$ (Mathlib's `IsAdicComplete`, which bundles both the Hausdorff property $\bigcap_n I^n = 0$ and surjectivity of the map to the inverse limit of the $R/I^n$), and assume that $I = (\varpi)$ is a maximal ideal of $R$. The conclusion is that $R$ is a discrete valuation ring in the sense of Mathlib's `IsDiscreteValuationRing`, i.e. $R$ is a local principal ideal domain that is not a field. No noetherian hypothesis is imposed: it is a consequence, as is the fact that the maximal ideal of $R$ is exactly $(\varpi)$ and that $\varpi$ is a uniformiser.
--
--   This is the standard characterisation of a complete discrete valuation ring by completeness with respect to a principal maximal ideal, in the form convenient for introducing a ring such as the completion of a maximal unramified extension axiomatically. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, in the analysis of the formal $\Omega$ and of the covering of the Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient by unit-locus charts.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsDiscreteValuationRing_of_isAdicComplete_span_singleton_of_isMaximal.lean

